      "name": "candidate_variant_a",
      "description": "ca1_reactive_lymphocyte_neighbor_fraction_r96px: fraction of CA1 pyramidal neurons with at least one CA1 reactive astrocyte and at least one CA1 lymphocyte within 96 px."
    },
    {
      "name": "candidate_variant_b",
      "description": "ca1_reactive_lymphocyte_neighbor_fraction_r128px: same metric with a 128 px neighborhood to test a slightly broader immune niche."
    }
  ],
  "baseline_variation": "candidate_variant_a",
  "notes": "Use centroid-based Euclidean neighbor queries in the cell-coordinate space; region restriction should be CA1 for anchor and neighbor cell types. Annotation polygons are stored at 16x cell coordinates and must be divided by 16 before point-in-polygon assignment. This feature does not require contour areas. If CA1 pyramidal count is extremely low, handle gracefully but avoid donor-specific rules.",
  "rationale": "The accepted panel has kept improving by refining the same CA1 reactive-pyramidal injury niche, but the last two gains came from adding supportive or debris-associated context within that niche and were modest. The most coherent next step is to test whether an explicit immune-cell component identifies a more damaging inflammatory subtype of the reactive CA1 niche. Lymphocytes are biologically distinct from oligodendrocyte loss and corpora amylacea accumulation, so this candidate has a plausible chance to add new signal or replace one of the weaker late-round niche refinements."
